Z||000000||SeanyD||06-28-2000||04:40 PM||||my 84 currentlyhas this great feature for the driverside window that it only goes down if the door is wide open....it's been this way for awhile...and it's only a minor problem compared to some of the other stuff...but have you ever tried opening the window with the button where it is while driving 80-90 km/h down the highway while having to hold the door open with your foot???||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||06-28-2000||04:47 PM||||Nope.  Haven't tried that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>Doesn't sound like fun.  I suppose if you wanted to fix it, you'd look for a break in the wiring to the driver's side door, near the hinges.   <P>On the other hand, if you like it that way...<P>||||reg||7
Z||000002||SeanyD||06-28-2000||04:51 PM||||no no....don't like it that way!! through towns easy because it's lower speeds ...but it's a standard so ya....it there a ground or something inside the door?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||06-28-2000||06:04 PM||||No ground in the door.  All of the wiring runs through the front of the door into the passenger compartment.  When you open your door, you should see the rubber boot that encases the wiring.  Assuming the window will rise with the door closed, it seems like it must be a broken wire.  It's not that difficult to remove the inner door panel to check.  On the other hand, it's a real b**** to get to the wiring on the inside of the car.  Start with the door and work your way in.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SeanyD||06-29-2000||01:22 AM||||thanks, I'll have a look at that and see what I can dig up out of the door!||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||06-29-2000||02:14 PM||||this circuit only has 2 wires. It works ok in one direction and not the other so in this case I don't think it's a wire problem. If it was a broken wire it would likely not work in either direction.<P>I think it's an adjustment problem. The door itself will flex when it's opened and closed. This can be just enough to trow off the window tracks. I think you are going to have to get inside the door and see why it's binding. <P>one thing to look for is make sure the window glass is tight to the lift hardware attached to it. Play in the lifting hardware can let the glass twist in the tracks.<P>It's also posible that the switch is crapped up. It may not be getting good conection in the up side contacts. A reduced voltage from the switch combined with even a slight bind would be enough to make the window act weird. ((Even tho the window is counter sprung it takes a little more power to raise it than lower it.))<P>The easiest way to test for that is pull down the carpet so you can get to the window power connector in the A post. It's the biggest connector with only 2 heavy wires.<P>stick a lenght of wire in each terminal on the door half of the connector and connect them to a strong 12 volt source. (This circuit can draw 10-15 amps per motor. It's fused for 30amps.) If you test using a car battery beware that this test WILL cause sparks. I like to use one of those emergency starting batteries for this. They have their own clamps and don't out gas anything flamable.<P>If it's the switch you'll now get the window to work fine. If the window comes up noticably slower than it goes down then it's binding. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula||06-29-2000||11:22 PM||||I thought that was a child safety option......hmmmmmmmmmmm||||reg||1
Z||000007||webbee||07-01-2000||03:53 AM||||Another little trick is to spray the tracks with silicone lube. I think it's a switch problem. Try spraying electronic contact cleaner in the switch contacts. Let it dry before testing. You can probably get it at Radio Shack. ||||reg||7
